  "notes": "NO HEALTHY-YET-COLLAPSING REGIME FOUND. KEY FINDINGS: (1) ALL LRs in {1e-4..1e-2} produce HEALTHY networks after Task 1 — dead_after_task1 ranges from 19.9% (lr=0.01) to 26.2% (lr=0.001), all well below the 30% threshold. (2) NONE of the 15 runs (5 LRs x 3 seeds) show plasticity collapse — new-task accuracy remains 25-79% throughout all 10 tasks, never dropping to chance (20%). Notably, final-task accuracy (last 3 tasks mean) is HIGHER than task-1 accuracy at all LRs (e.g., lr=0.01: task1=60.7% → final=66.8%), indicating the trunk actually IMPROVES from multi-task exposure. (3) CONTRAST with prior rounds: the baseline/precedence experiments used lr=0.05 (Permuted CIFAR-10, SHARED head) where dead_after_task1=76.7% and collapse was rapid. The two critical differences are: (a) the task setting (task-incremental with fresh 5-class heads prevents collapse regardless of trunk health — even a degraded trunk suffices for a fresh head on 5 classes), and (b) the much lower LR range tested here avoids dying-ReLU entirely. (4) CONCLUSION: In the Split-CIFAR-100 task-incremental setting, there is NO lr where the model is healthy at task 1 AND still loses plasticity over 10 tasks. Collapse does not occur at any LR in this grid. The prior round's collapse (lr=0.05, Permuted CIFAR-10, shared head) combined two confounds: dying-ReLU pathology AND the harder shared-head setting. The lrgrid result does NOT definitively rule out genuine plasticity collapse — it rules out collapse in THIS specific (task-incremental, 10-task, Split-CIFAR-100) setting. Recommended action: to study genuine healthy-regime collapse, use Permuted CIFAR-10 (shared head, 20+ tasks) at a lower LR like 3e-3 or 1e-2 — but that experiment was not run here."
